// Seller Home — Knowledge page data
// =====================================================================
// Consumed by src/views/knowledge.html (which composes _home_left_nav
// via the .seller-home shell). gulpfile.js's getData() auto-merges
// this file into the template's local scope by filename → template lookup.
//
// This file intentionally carries ONLY page-specific bindings. Global
// nav content (navPrimary, navFooter, recents) is pulled at template
// time from home.json via the `readData("home").<key>` Nunjucks
// global registered in gulpfile.js — the Knowledge page is a sub-
// surface of the Seller Home nav, so both pages must show the same
// primary items and recents list without duplication.
//
// Fields:
//   • pageTitle          (string) window title + <main aria-label>
//   • navActive          (string) id of the currently-active left-nav
//                        item. The renderNavItem macro in
//                        _home_left_nav.html applies `.is-active` +
//                        `aria-current="page"` when this equals
//                        item.id. "knowledge" matches the renamed
//                        entry in home.json → navPrimary.
//   • newKnowledgeLabel  (string) label for the header CTA button.
//                        Kept as data so future localization + a
//                        possible label change ("Add document",
//                        "New file") happens without a template edit.
//   • header             (object) misc header content:
//                        - heading   (string) H1 displayed above the
//                                    document list; matches the SLDS
//                                    Titles/font-scale-3 treatment
//                                    from the Figma frame 8931:383002.
//   • search             (object) copy for the doc-list search field
//                        (Figma frame 8931:383736 → "Input - Search").
//                        Filtering is client-only (see home.js →
//                        bindKnowledgeSearch): trimmed, case-
//                        insensitive substring match against each
//                        row's `name` + `type`. Fields:
//                          - label         (string) accessible label
//                                          for the input (rendered as
//                                          `slds-assistive-text` for a
//                                          visually iconic input that
//                                          still names itself to a
//                                          screen reader).
//                          - placeholder   (string) input placeholder.
//                          - clearLabel    (string) accessible label
//                                          + tooltip for the clear (×)
//                                          button that surfaces when
//                                          the field has a value.
//   • emptyState         (object) copy for the "no matches" state
//                        shown in the doc-list slot when a search
//                        query filters out every row. Kept as data so
//                        the same partial can be reused for a future
//                        "no documents yet" first-run state with
//                        different copy. Fields:
//                          - title         (string) primary heading
//                                          for the empty state.
//                          - description   (string) supporting text
//                                          below the heading.
//   • documents          (array) doc-list rows rendered by
//                        _home_knowledge_list.html → renderKnowledgeDocRow.
//                        Each item is a stable-shaped record so a
//                        future API can drop in without template edits.
//                        Fields:
//                          - id         (string, required) URL-safe,
//                                       collision-free identifier used
//                                       as the DOM hook (`data-sh-
//                                       knowledge-doc-id`), the menu's
//                                       id-suffix, and the future
//                                       routing key for the doc detail
//                                       view. Keep kebab-case.
//                          - name       (string, required) primary text
//                                       shown on the row. Long names
//                                       ellipsize via CSS; the full
//                                       string is exposed through the
//                                       row's native `title` tooltip.
//                          - type       (string, required) high-level
//                                       taxonomy label displayed in the
//                                       row's secondary metadata line
//                                       (e.g. "Document"). Kept as data
//                                       so a future doc kind ("Skill
//                                       File", "Playbook") reads through
//                                       without template edits.
//                          - fileType   (string, required) sprite id in
//                                       /assets/icons/doctype-sprite/svg/
//                                       symbols.svg → picks the yellow
//                                       doctype icon (txt / pdf / …).
//                                       Must be one of the sprites SLDS
//                                       ships; unknown values render a
//                                       blank <use> and swallow the
//                                       icon visually.
//                          - updatedAt  (string, required) preformatted
//                                       "MMM D, YYYY" date shown after
//                                       the type on the metadata line.
//                                       Preformatted here (not a raw
//                                       date) so the render path stays
//                                       locale-agnostic; a future i18n
//                                       phase can move this into a
//                                       date-string producer.
//                          - updatedBy  (string, optional) display name
//                                       of the last editor. Rendered on
//                                       the document-detail page as
//                                       "Last edited by <updatedBy> ·
//                                       <updatedAt>". Omitted from the
//                                       list row itself so the compact
//                                       row shape stays unchanged.
//                          - markdown   (string, required for the
//                                       detail page) full document body
//                                       in the same subset the shared
//                                       `KnowledgeDocumentView` module
//                                       parses (headings `#`/`##`/`###`,
//                                       paragraphs, `-` ul, `1.` ol,
//                                       inline `**bold**`, `*italic*`,
//                                       `` `code` ``, `[link](url)`).
//                                       The detail page reads this
//                                       field client-side by matching
//                                       the row's id against
//                                       `?id=<id>` on the URL.
//                          - href       (string, optional) target URL
//                                       for the row's anchor. Omit or
//                                       leave empty and the row
//                                       template defaults to
//                                       `/knowledge_document_detail.html
//                                       ?id=<id>`, which is the built-in
//                                       Seller Home detail page. Set an
//                                       explicit `href` only when a
//                                       specific doc should route
//                                       somewhere else (a legacy URL,
//                                       an external link, etc.).
//   • documentActions    (array) menu items shown in every doc row's
//                        overflow menu (⋮). Rendered by the row macro
//                        in the same order they appear here, so
//                        reordering is a data-only edit. Fields:
//                          - id     (string, required) action slug
//                                   emitted in `sh:knowledge-doc-
//                                   action` details (`{action, docId}`).
//                                   Kebab-case, stable across releases.
//                          - label  (string, required) visible menu-
//                                   item text.
//                          - icon   (string, optional) utility-sprite id
//                                   for the item's leading glyph. Same
//                                   pattern the review-row overflow uses.
//
// Phase 4 landed: search input (Figma 8931:383736) filters the doc
// list client-side against `documents[*].name` + `documents[*].type`
// and swaps the list for an `emptyState`-driven "no matches" view
// when nothing survives the query. Row-level overflow menu, doc
// list, and mock-data-driven rendering are also in from Phase 3.
//
// Phase 5 landed: each document now carries a full markdown body +
// author. Rows link to `/knowledge_document_detail.html?id=<id>` by
// default (template-side fallback in `_home_knowledge_list.html`);
// the detail page reads the SAME `documents[]` catalog via Nunjucks
// `readData("knowledge")` so there's exactly one source of truth for
// document data — no duplicated JSON, no drift between list + detail.
{
  "pageTitle": "Knowledge",
  "navActive": "knowledge",
  "newKnowledgeLabel": "New Knowledge",
  "header": {
    "heading": "Knowledge"
  },
  "search": {
    "label": "Search knowledge",
    "placeholder": "Search...",
    "clearLabel": "Clear search"
  },
  "emptyState": {
    "title": "No knowledge found",
    "description": "Try a different search term."
  },
  "documentActions": [
    { "id": "download", "label": "Download", "icon": "download" },
    { "id": "delete",   "label": "Delete",   "icon": "delete"   }
  ],
  "documents": [
    {
      "id": "customer-discovery-skill-file",
      "name": "Auto-Learning from Your Past Customer Interactions",
      "type": "Document",
      "fileType": "txt",
      "updatedAt": "Jul 28, 2026",
      "updatedBy": "Issa Mao",
      "markdown": "# Auto-Learning from Your Past Customer Interactions\n\n## Q1: What Is Your Ideal Customer Profile?\n\n### ICP Dimension 1: B2B SaaS / B2B Software Company\n\n**Frequency:** 9/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** High\n\nEvery opportunity in the dataset is a B2B software company. This is the foundational firmographic qualifier.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. Mixpanel: Enterprise B2B SaaS with dedicated demand generation team and multi-region SDR coverage\n2. TravelPerk: Mid-market SaaS with BDR+SE team structure, 45-day sales cycle\n3. Udemy: B2B SaaS with global BDR teams, ~$25K ACV, 40 seats across engagement tools\n4. Zone & Co: FinTech B2B company transitioning from inbound to outbound\n\n### ICP Dimension 2: Sizeable SDR/BDR Team (15+ Reps)\n\n**Frequency:** 7/9 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Branch, Diligent, Udemy, Optimizely, Movable Ink) | **Confidence:** High\n\nStrong signal: 7 of 9 opportunities have 15+ SDRs/BDRs. The product's value scales with team size.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. Diligent: 50-100+ BDRs across acquisitions\n2. Optimizely: 47 SDRs deployed globally\n3. Movable Ink: 25-30 SDRs, outbound-heavy\n4. Branch: 15+ BDRs, VC-backed mid-market SaaS\n\n### ICP Dimension 3: Outbound-Oriented Sales Motion\n\n**Frequency:** 5/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** High\n\nOutbound-heavy or actively transitioning to outbound. The product's value proposition centers on automating outbound workflows.\n\n### ICP Dimension 4: Existing Intent/ABM Tool Investment (6Sense, Demandbase, UserGems)\n\n**Frequency:** 5/9 opportunities (Workato, Udemy, Optimizely, Zone & Co, Branch) | **Confidence:** High\n\nCustomers already invested in intent/ABM tools and looking to complement, consolidate, or replace them.\n\n### ICP Dimension 5: Uses Conversation Intelligence (Gong)\n\n**Frequency:** 5/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** Medium\n\n### ICP Dimension 6: Salesforce as Primary CRM\n\n**Frequency:** 5/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** Medium\n\n### ICP Dimension 7: Multi-Tool Sales Tech Stack (4+ Tools)\n\n**Frequency:** 5/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** High\n\nCustomers using 4+ GTM tools (ZoomInfo, Gong, Demandbase, 6Sense, Outreach, Nooks, etc.) — creating both the pain (tool sprawl) and the opportunity (consolidation).\n\n### ICP Dimension 8: Global / Multi-Region Sales Teams\n\n**Frequency:** 4/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** Medium\n\n### ICP Dimension 9: High Call Volume / Activity-Dense Pipeline\n\n**Frequency:** 4/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** Medium\n\n### ICP Dimension 10: Uses Sales Engagement Platform (Outreach/SalesLoft/Nooks)\n\n**Frequency:** 4/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** Medium\n\n### ICP Dimension 11: Uses ZoomInfo for Data Enrichment\n\n**Frequency:** 3/9 opportunities | **Confidence:** Medium\n\n## Q2: What Business Problem Is the Customer Trying to Solve?\n\n### Theme 1: Manual Prospecting & SDR Productivity Drain\n\n**Frequency:** 12/22 transcripts across 6 opportunities (TravelPerk, Branch, Diligent, Udemy, Optimizely, Movable Ink) | **Confidence:** High\n\nThe dominant problem. SDR/BDR teams spend disproportionate time on manual research, list building, and contact sourcing — directly competing with selling time. Reps toggle between multiple tools to build prospect lists instead of running pipeline.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Reps spend too much time on manual prospecting and admin instead of selling\" — TravelPerk (Customer)\n2. \"SDRs manually building prospect lists across multiple tools, significant tool fatigue\" — Branch (Customer)\n3. \"Reps waste time across five-plus tools for manual prospecting\" — Udemy (Customer)\n4. \"SDRs spend excessive time manually sourcing contacts on Sales Navigator\" — Optimizely (Customer)\n\n### Theme 2: CRM & Data Quality Degradation\n\n**Frequency:** 6/22 transcripts across 4 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Optimizely, Zone & Co) | **Confidence:** High\n\nDirty, inconsistent, or broken CRM data undermines downstream workflows — from incorrect field values to integration failures and M&A migration issues. Both a standalone pain and a root cause that amplifies other themes.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Three hundred-plus accounts had the wrong country assigned in CRM\" — TravelPerk (Customer)\n2. \"Salesforce write errors from leads-to-contacts migration, industry taxonomy inconsistency\" — TravelPerk (Customer)\n3. \"Broken data integrations between Salesforce, Outreach, and ZoomInfo\" — Mixpanel (Customer)\n4. \"Manual painful contact enrichment, CRM data cleanliness is a constant struggle\" — Zone & Co (Customer)\n\n### Theme 3: Pipeline Generation Under Pressure\n\n**Frequency:** 5/22 transcripts across 4 opportunities (Mixpanel, Branch, Diligent, Movable Ink) | **Confidence:** High\n\nTeams failing to generate or maintain sufficient pipeline — outbound campaigns producing zero results, smaller teams expected to maintain output, headcount scaling hitting diminishing returns.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Zero pipeline generated from twenty-eight hundred emails sent\" — Mixpanel (Customer)\n2. \"Maintain pipeline with a smaller SDR team, need to automate lower-tier outreach\" — Movable Ink (Customer)\n3. \"Diminishing returns from headcount additions, deal fell through due to internal blockers\" — Diligent (Seller)\n4. \"Pipeline issues, need fast time-to-value, avoid shelfware risk\" — Branch (Customer)\n\n### Theme 4: Tool Sprawl & Fragmentation\n\n**Frequency:** 4/22 transcripts across 3 opportunities (Branch, Udemy, Optimizely) | **Confidence:** High\n\nGTM stacks have accumulated 4-6+ overlapping tools with no unified layer, each adding friction and no single tool providing a consolidated view.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Need to consolidate GTM tools and reduce SDR admin and research time\" — Branch (Customer)\n2. \"Reps waste time across five-plus tools for manual prospecting workflows\" — Udemy (Customer)\n3. \"Four separate 6sense models with no cross-prioritization across business units\" — Optimizely (Customer)\n\n### Theme 5: Account Prioritization & Scoring Model Gaps\n\n**Frequency:** 4/22 transcripts across 3 opportunities (Workato, Diligent, Optimizely) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nExisting intent-scoring and account-prioritization models are underutilized, poorly configured, or absent — conflating fit with intent signals, lacking segment-specific models.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Team not leveraging intent scoring fully, scoring model mixes fit and signal fields\" — Workato (Customer)\n2. \"Need separate enterprise scoring model, want to validate Bluebirds versus Demandbase data\" — Workato (Customer)\n3. \"BDR low connection rates, cannot prioritize accounts effectively\" — Diligent (Customer)\n\n## Q3: Why Are They Meeting with Us Now?\n\n### Theme 1: Quarterly / End-of-Period Pressure & Pipeline Targets\n\n**Frequency:** 8/22 transcripts across 5 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Branch, Diligent, Udemy) | **Confidence:** High\n\nFiscal quarter boundaries, QBR accountability, and pipeline shortfalls as the forcing function. Urgency tied to leadership visibility and measurable targets.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"End of Q2 pressure, QBRs, leadership asking for ROI, only 900 of 3,000 leads sent\" — Mixpanel (Seller)\n2. \"End-of-quarter urgency, pilot launch for BDR productivity\" — TravelPerk (Customer)\n3. \"Want tool before end of Q3, fully operational by Q4\" — Branch (Customer)\n4. \"BDR charter underperforming, need to boost Q3 numbers\" — Diligent (Seller)\n\n### Theme 2: Imminent Launch Deadline or Time-Bound Deliverable\n\n**Frequency:** 6/22 transcripts across 4 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Workato, Branch) | **Confidence:** High\n\nConcrete near-term deadlines — pilot go-lives, rebranding cutovers, POC windows — creating urgency beyond general quarter pressure.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Upcoming September rebranding deadline\" — Mixpanel (Customer)\n2. \"Messaging approved, want leads flowing by midweek, 500 leads per week pressure\" — Mixpanel (Customer)\n3. \"Pilot launching Monday\" — TravelPerk (Customer)\n4. \"Re-evaluating target account list, need POC within one week\" — Workato (Customer)\n\n### Theme 3: Strategic Leadership Change or GTM Motion Shift\n\n**Frequency:** 5/22 transcripts across 4 opportunities (Movable Ink, Zone & Co, Optimizely, Branch) | **Confidence:** High\n\nNew leaders arrive with mandates to shift GTM — typically inbound-only or partner-led to outbound-first. Leadership transitions create budget unlocks and urgency.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"New SDR leader 8 months in, peer recommendations\" — Movable Ink (Customer)\n2. \"New leadership team transitioning GTM from partner-driven to outbound\" — Zone & Co (Customer)\n3. \"Company-wide AI initiative, leadership loosened spending\" — Optimizely (Customer)\n4. \"Record inbound quarter but lower ACV, need outbound for bigger deals\" — Branch (Seller)\n\n### Theme 4: Vendor Evaluation Complete / Advancing to POC\n\n**Frequency:** 4/22 transcripts across 4 opportunities (Udemy, Optimizely, Workato, Diligent) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nCustomers have done their research, narrowed the field, and are meeting now to move to proof-of-concept or contracting.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Finished vendor eval, prioritizing POC\" — Udemy (Customer)\n2. \"Completed POC review, internal AI effort stalled\" — Optimizely (Customer)\n3. \"LOI signed but internal dynamics shifted\" — Diligent (Customer)\n\n### Theme 5: Technical Blockers / Integration Failures Forcing Action\n\n**Frequency:** 4/22 transcripts across 3 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Optimizely) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nSomething is actively broken — integrations failing, data quality blocking a launch, internal build that stalled. Triage calls, not exploratory.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Need to fix broken integrations urgently\" — Mixpanel (Customer)\n2. \"Data quality issues blocking launch\" — TravelPerk (Customer)\n3. \"Blocked on integration errors and taxonomy migration\" — TravelPerk (Customer)\n4. \"Internal AI effort stalled\" — Optimizely (Customer)\n\n### Theme 6: Team Readiness — Internal Stakeholders Finally Aligned\n\n**Frequency:** 3/22 transcripts across 2 opportunities (Workato, Udemy) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nInterest was present but blocked by internal readiness — now demand gen is staffed, prospecting motion defined, or stakeholders aligned.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"DG team finally ready to action intent signals\" — Workato (Customer)\n2. \"Q3 dedicated to solving prospecting motion\" — Udemy (Customer)\n3. \"Re-evaluating target account list, need POC within one week\" — Workato (Customer)\n\n## Q4: What Use Case Are They Interested In?\n\n### Theme 1: Automated List Building & AI Prospecting\n\n**Frequency:** 11/22 transcripts across 7 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Branch, Diligent, Udemy, Movable Ink, Zone & Co) | **Confidence:** High\n\nThe dominant use case. AI agent automatically builds target account/contact lists on a recurring basis (typically weekly), with enrichment data attached, ready to push into a sales engagement platform.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Weekly automated prospecting at scale, a hundred leads per rep per week\" — TravelPerk (Seller)\n2. \"Automated weekly list building for BDR cold calling, that's the core ask\" — Diligent (Seller)\n3. \"AI prospecting plus list building with enrichment flowing into Salesforce and Gong Engage\" — Udemy (Seller)\n4. \"AI agent identifies accounts and contacts, enriches with signals, drafts messaging, pushes to Outreach\" — TravelPerk (Seller)\n\n### Theme 2: Data Enrichment & CRM/SEP Integration\n\n**Frequency:** 7/22 transcripts across 6 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Branch, Udemy, Optimizely, Zone & Co) | **Confidence:** High\n\nCustomers call out the need for enrichment (contact data, technographics, intent signals) and seamless integration into Salesforce and sales engagement platforms. CRM cleanup and automated contact creation are recurring sub-themes.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Lead gen plus enrichment flowing into Outreach, and Salesforce campaign attribution\" — Mixpanel (Seller)\n2. \"CRM integration for automated contact creation plus Outreach sequencing\" — TravelPerk (Customer)\n3. \"Automated outbound list building, data enrichment, Demandbase operationalization\" — Branch (Seller)\n4. \"Contact enrichment, outbound support, gifting triggered by job changes\" — Zone & Co (Customer)\n\n### Theme 3: Multi-Channel Outbound Execution (Email, Phone, LinkedIn)\n\n**Frequency:** 5/22 transcripts across 4 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Branch, Diligent) | **Confidence:** High\n\nOutbound execution across multiple channels — not just email. Phone/cold calling, LinkedIn connection requests, and LinkedIn ads are explicitly mentioned.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Multi-channel outbound — email, LinkedIn ads, LinkedIn connection requests, cold calling via Nooks\" — Mixpanel (Customer)\n2. \"Strategic pivot to simpler play-based sequencing, BDR phone and LinkedIn over email\" — Mixpanel (Seller)\n3. \"Phone connection optimization, that's where they pivoted to TitanX\" — Diligent (Seller)\n\n### Theme 4: Account Prioritization & Intent Scoring\n\n**Frequency:** 4/22 transcripts across 2 opportunities (Workato, Optimizely) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nScoring and ranking accounts using technographics, growth signals, and CIO presence. Includes unifying multiple scoring models and surfacing unassigned accounts.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Update intent scoring model plus job change campaign for enterprise targets\" — Workato (Customer)\n2. \"Separate enterprise scoring model with technographics, CIO presence, growth rate\" — Workato (Seller)\n3. \"Account prioritization plus finding the right people from CRM and LinkedIn\" — Optimizely (Customer)\n4. \"Unified cross-model account prioritization, unassigned account surfacing, CRM cleanup\" — Optimizely (Seller)\n\n### Theme 5: AI-Personalized Messaging & Outreach Drafting\n\n**Frequency:** 3/22 transcripts across 3 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Movable Ink) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nAI drafts personalized outreach messages using buyer signals and persona mapping, including handling lower-tier accounts with AI-generated outreach.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Agent-powered personalized outbound email using signals plus persona mapping\" — Mixpanel (Seller)\n2. \"AI agent drafts messaging based on enriched signals, pushes directly to Outreach\" — TravelPerk (Seller)\n3. \"AI account research and targeting, handle lower-tier outreach automatically\" — Movable Ink (Customer)\n\n### Theme 6: Closed-Lost Re-engagement & Win-Back Plays\n\n**Frequency:** 3/22 transcripts across 3 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Branch) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nUsing AI to mine closed-lost opportunities (often via Gong call transcript analysis) to identify re-engagement signals and run win-back campaigns.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Closed-loss re-engagement via call transcripts, mining those for signals\" — Mixpanel (Customer)\n2. \"AI lead gen plus closed-lost re-engagement via Gong transcript analysis\" — TravelPerk (Seller)\n3. \"Closed-loss play, tech rip-and-replace targeting competitive displacements\" — Branch (Seller)\n\n## Q5: How Do Our Sellers Articulate the Value Prop?\n\n### Theme 1: Time Savings & Automation of Rep Prospecting\n\n**Frequency:** 7/23 transcripts across 5 opportunities (TravelPerk, Branch, Optimizely, Movable Ink, Udemy) | **Confidence:** High\n\nSellers frame the product as eliminating manual, time-intensive BDR/SDR work, quantifying savings in concrete percentages (30–90%).\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Reduce prospecting from 100% to ~20% of rep time, automate 80-90%\" — TravelPerk (Seller)\n2. \"Automates 30-50% of rep list-building, LinkedIn-quality enrichment\" — Branch (Seller)\n3. \"AI agent replicates best SDR's nuance at scale, reads full LinkedIn profiles\" — Optimizely (Seller)\n4. \"Automates research/targeting bottleneck — 50% of BDR time — prioritized lists with verified data\" — Movable Ink (Seller)\n\n### Theme 2: Intelligent Targeting & ICP Scoring\n\n**Frequency:** 7/23 transcripts across 3 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Workato) | **Confidence:** High\n\nICP scoring and signal-based targeting positioned as the core value driver — not personalization, but getting in front of the right accounts.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Core value is targeting — ICP scoring, signals — not message personalization, 80% success through targeting\" — Mixpanel (Seller)\n2. \"Finding best accounts via scoring plus signals, personalized messaging, push to existing workflow tools\" — TravelPerk (Seller)\n3. \"Map TAM/SAM through weighted scoring, combine firmographic fit plus intent signals\" — Workato (Seller)\n4. \"Self-service targeting configuration, FIT score for LinkedIn ad spend optimization\" — Mixpanel (Seller)\n\n### Theme 3: Superior Enrichment & Data Quality\n\n**Frequency:** 6/23 transcripts across 5 opportunities (Branch, Diligent, Udemy, Zone & Co, Optimizely) | **Confidence:** High\n\nWaterfall enrichment across 15 vendors, LinkedIn-sourced data quality, and measurably low bounce rates (sub-1% to sub-2%) as differentiation proof points.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Best-in-class enrichment, in-house AI, waterfall enrichment 15 vendors, sub-1% bounce\" — Udemy (Seller)\n2. \"Waterfall enrichment via 15 vendors — BDRs never learn new tool, intelligent list building\" — Diligent (Seller)\n3. \"LinkedIn-sourced enrichment, sub-2% bounce rate\" — Zone & Co (Seller)\n4. \"Reps wake up Monday with prioritized lists, sub-2% bounce rate, 1-month onboarding\" — Branch (Seller)\n\n### Theme 4: Tool Consolidation & Vendor Displacement\n\n**Frequency:** 6/23 transcripts across 5 opportunities (Branch, Diligent, Workato, Udemy, Zone & Co) | **Confidence:** High\n\nThe platform replaces multiple point solutions (Demandbase, ZoomInfo, Common Room), enabling cost savings and reducing stack complexity.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Cut Demandbase licenses, automate workflow from intent to sequencing\" — Workato (Seller)\n2. \"AI-native vs bolt-on AI — Common Room — transparent vs black box — Actively — tool consolidation\" — Branch (Seller)\n3. \"Leverage against ZoomInfo, tool consolidation\" — Udemy (Seller)\n4. \"Consolidate point solutions, unlimited predictive models\" — Zone & Co (Seller)\n\n### Theme 5: Native CRM & Workflow Integration\n\n**Frequency:** 5/23 transcripts across 4 opportunities (Mixpanel, TravelPerk, Workato, Diligent) | **Confidence:** High\n\nProduct works inside existing tools (Salesforce first, then Outreach/HubSpot), requiring no new UI. Setup friction minimized (30-min install, dedup checks built in).\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Seamless integration — Salesforce first then Outreach — dedup checks, respects existing workflows\" — TravelPerk (Seller)\n2. \"Native Salesforce integration eliminates need for separate tools\" — Workato (Seller)\n3. \"Low-risk integration, 30-min setup, opt-out clause, contained scope\" — Diligent (Seller)\n4. \"Automated lead gen engine, self-serve configuration, Salesforce campaign integration\" — Mixpanel (Seller)\n\n### Theme 6: AI-Native Differentiation vs Competitors\n\n**Frequency:** 3/23 transcripts across 3 opportunities (Branch, Diligent, Udemy) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nSellers contrast the product's AI architecture against competitors — \"AI-native vs bolt-on\", \"transparent vs black box\", \"in-house AI\" — positioning technical superiority as trust and performance differentiator.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"AI-native vs bolt-on AI — Common Room — transparent vs black box — Actively\" — Branch (Seller)\n2. \"Trusted advisor, complementary positioning with competitors\" — Diligent (Seller)\n3. \"Best-in-class enrichment, in-house AI, mirrors best SDR\" — Udemy (Seller)\n\n### Theme 7: Low Risk & Easy Onboarding\n\n**Frequency:** 3/23 transcripts across 3 opportunities (Diligent, Branch, TravelPerk) | **Confidence:** Medium\n\nSellers lower perceived risk with short onboarding windows, opt-out clauses, contained scope, and learning from feedback.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n1. \"Low-risk integration, 30-min setup, opt-out clause, contained scope\" — Diligent (Seller)\n2. \"Sub-2% bounce rate, 1-month onboarding, no new tool\" — Branch (Seller)\n3. \"Automate 80-90%, learn from feedback\" — TravelPerk (Seller)\n\n## Appendix: Weak Signals\n\n### Q1 Weak Signals\n\n- **AI Agent / Automated Messaging Quality** (3 transcripts, 1 opp — Mixpanel only): AI agent mixing up persona messaging, QA bottlenecks. Single-opp signal.\n- **Internal Organizational Blockers** (2 transcripts, 1 opp — Diligent only): Internal politics and failed prior implementations blocking tool adoption.\n\n### Q2 Weak Signals\n\n- **Bluebirds Acquisition by Salesforce** (2 transcripts, 2 opps): Platform transition as urgency context, not standalone trigger.\n- **Post-Loss Debrief / Underperformance Recovery** (2 transcripts, 1 opp — Diligent): Previous tool/initiative failed, customer now receptive.\n- **Peer Recommendation / Word of Mouth** (1 transcript, 1 opp — Movable Ink): Peer referral as trigger — likely underreported.\n\n### Q3 Weak Signals\n\n- **Job Change / Signal-Based Triggers** (2 transcripts, 2 opps — Workato, Zone & Co): Using job change events for gifting campaigns and scoring model enrichment.\n\n### Q4 Weak Signals\n\n- **Speed-to-Value / Rapid Time-to-First-Lead** (1 transcript — Mixpanel): \"48-hour lead generation\" as proof point.\n- **Messaging Framework Simplification** (1 transcript — Mixpanel): Product simplifies how messaging is built.\n- **Pricing as Competitive Lever** (1 transcript — Udemy): Pricing model positioned as weapon against incumbents.\n- **Unlimited Predictive Models** (1 transcript — Zone & Co): Unlimited model capacity as differentiator.\n\n### Q5 Weak Signals\n\n- **Messy CRM from M&A / Acquisitions** (2 opps — Diligent, Optimizely): M&A-driven CRM data quality issues as a situational trigger.\n- **VC-Backed Growth Stage** (1 opp — Branch): VC-backed mid-market as growth-stage signal.\n- **Inbound-to-Outbound Transition** (1 opp — Zone & Co): Actively transitioning from 70-80% inbound to outbound.\n\n## Cross-Cutting Observations\n\n1. **Targeting > Messaging:** Sellers consistently lead with targeting/scoring as the primary value driver, with messaging/personalization secondary. As one seller put it: \"80% success through targeting + BDR follow-up.\"\n2. **\"No new tool\" is the meta-theme:** Tool Consolidation, CRM Integration, and Low Risk all orbit a single buyer objection — \"I don't want another tool.\" Sellers address it from multiple angles.\n3. **Use cases form a natural funnel:** Account Prioritization → Automated List Building → Data Enrichment & CRM Integration → AI-Personalized Messaging → Multi-Channel Outbound Execution. Most customers want 2-3 capabilities bundled, with list building as the anchor (7/9 opps).\n4. **Quarter pressure is universal:** Fiscal quarter boundaries and QBR accountability appear as the #1 trigger event (5 opps, 8 transcripts). Time the outreach cycle accordingly.\n5. **Competitor positioning varies by deal stage:** Named competitors (Common Room, Actively, Demandbase, ZoomInfo) appear in later-stage deals; earlier-stage calls use generic framing.\n6. **ICP sweet spot:** B2B SaaS with 15+ BDRs, Salesforce-based, already invested in 4+ GTM tools, outbound-oriented or transitioning to outbound. These companies have both the pain (manual prospecting, tool sprawl) and the infrastructure (CRM, SEP) to integrate immediately.\n\n## Footer: Parameter Snapshot for Deterministic Re-Run\n\n- **run_id:** `9e2b819a-5f71-413f-b95b-4362df9ee1e1`\n- **plan_version:** 1\n- **lookback_days:** 90\n- **opp_cap:** 10\n- **transcripts_per_opp:** 4\n- **min_theme_support:** 3\n- **source:** Salesforce VoiceCall → ConversationEntry (via ConversationId)\n- **opportunities_analyzed:** 9 (of 10 cap; Tipalti excluded — no-show)\n- **transcripts_read:** 23 (of 24 fetched)\n- **skill_key:** `customer_discovery_skill_v1`\n\n## Execution Log\n\n- **Task 1:** ✅ 95 qualifying accounts → 31 VoiceCalls → top 10 opportunities selected\n- **Tasks 2-3:** ✅ 24 transcripts fetched and analyzed across 10 opportunities; 23 usable (1 no-show excluded from Tipalti)\n- **Task 4:** ✅ Results stored incrementally per opportunity\n- **Task 5:** ✅ 5 parallel synthesis sub-passes completed (one per question)\n- **Task 6:** ✅ Skill file assembled and saved to durable state (key: `customer_discovery_skill_v1`)\n- **Skipped actions:** `save_skill` tool not available in environment; skill persisted via `set_item_state` only\n"
